/* ============================================================
   SOLENIS ACCOUNT LAYER. Carried verbatim from the working
   Solenis studio deployment (brief of 16 June 2026). Content
   is not rewritten here; only the container changed.
   ============================================================ */

var ACCOUNT_SOLENIS = {};
ACCOUNT_SOLENIS.company = "Solenis";
ACCOUNT_SOLENIS.briefDate = "16 June 2026";
ACCOUNT_SOLENIS.wedgesInSummary = true;
ACCOUNT_SOLENIS.summaryHtml = "<div class=\"sx\"><h1>Account summary</h1>\n      <div class=\"srcnote\"><b>Source.</b> This picture is built only from the reworked CNR account brief dated 16 June 2026, which was error checked for accuracy. Figures are refreshed only when new data appears on the same points from Solenis company sources or reputable analyst reports. Where the public record is silent, the tool says so and treats the gap as a question to ask, not a number to state.</div>\n\n      <p class=\"lead\">Solenis is not a generic specialty chemicals company. It is a sponsor-owned platform, built through acquisition, that uses scale to combine water treatment, hygiene, process chemistries, digital monitoring and application-specific solutions across paper and packaging, institutional hygiene, food and beverage, industrial water, pools and adjacent industrial markets. For us, the clearest near-term opening is European paper-based food-contact packaging, where new rules, a visible Solenis product set and a real buyer all line up. The job is to help Solenis decide where to focus first.</p>\n\n      <div class=\"stats\">\n        <div class=\"stat\"><div class=\"big\">23,000+</div><div class=\"lab\">employees, the scale of the company after the Diversey and NCH acquisitions</div></div>\n        <div class=\"stat\"><div class=\"big\">78</div><div class=\"lab\">manufacturing facilities worldwide</div></div>\n        <div class=\"stat\"><div class=\"big\">160+</div><div class=\"lab\">countries with Solenis operations</div></div>\n        <div class=\"stat\"><div class=\"big\">Platinum Equity</div><div class=\"lab\">private owner since 2021. Bain Capital and the Levy family hold minority stakes since the NCH deal</div></div>\n      </div>\n      <p class=\"srcline\">Sources. Solenis Quick Facts and Our History, company pages. Current scale reflects the Diversey and NCH build-out, not older descriptions still online.</p>\n\n      <h2>What kind of company this is</h2>\n      <p class=\"muted\">The most useful thing to understand is that Solenis no longer has one centre of gravity. Its public website is organised by the markets it serves and the solution families it sells, not by a simple paper-chemicals story. This is the public, market-facing structure. It is not a financial breakdown, because Solenis does not publish how each part performs. The practical lesson is to start any conversation from the decision facing a specific market or application, not from a general pitch about the company.</p>\n      <div class=\"archgrid\">\n        <details class=\"segc\"><summary><div class=\"top\"><div><div class=\"nm\">The markets it serves</div><div class=\"src\">Public market routes</div></div></div><div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip2 red\">Consumer, paper and packaging</span><span class=\"chip2\">Food and beverage processing</span><span class=\"chip2\">Industrial</span><span class=\"chip2\">Institutional</span><span class=\"chip2\">Pool and spa</span></div><span class=\"more\"><span class=\"lbl\">Why this matters to us</span><span class=\"cv\"></span></span></summary><div class=\"why\">Our entry point sits inside the Consumer route, the part that covers paper and packaging chemistry. The other routes were added largely through acquisition and are not our lane for a first conversation. Knowing which route a person sits in tells us whether our paper and packaging story is relevant to them at all.</div></details>\n        <details class=\"segc\"><summary><div class=\"top\"><div><div class=\"nm\">What it sells</div><div class=\"src\">Public solution families</div></div></div><div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip2 red\">Pulp and paper</span><span class=\"chip2\">Water treatment</span><span class=\"chip2\">Commercial cleaning</span><span class=\"chip2\">Food and beverage production</span><span class=\"chip2\">Industrial processing</span><span class=\"chip2\">Digital</span></div><span class=\"more\"><span class=\"lbl\">Why this matters to us</span><span class=\"cv\"></span></span></summary><div class=\"why\">Barrier coatings for packaging sit within the pulp and paper family, alongside a growing digital monitoring offer. The breadth shows Solenis wants to be seen as a broad solutions partner, not only a supplier of chemicals, which is the frame our work should fit into.</div></details>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"implication\"><b>What this means for us.</b> Six themes recur across the public record, water stewardship, hygiene and food safety, digital monitoring, paper-based packaging and barrier coatings, sustainability and circularity, and industrial process efficiency. Buying decisions sit with market general managers, application heads, regional commercial leaders and innovation teams, not with one central innovation budget. So we target the leader closest to the specific decision, not the company in general.</div>\n      <p class=\"srcline\">Sources. Solenis market and product pages, and the 2025 sustainability report. The structure above is the public website architecture, not a legal or financial org chart.</p>\n\n      <h2>How Solenis got to this scale, by buying other companies</h2>\n      <p class=\"muted\">Solenis has grown by acquiring a series of businesses and building them into a broader platform. The pattern is deliberate. Each deal added the ability to cross-sell chemistry, service, monitoring and compliance support into a neighbouring operational problem. The list is in date order. A price is shown only where Solenis announced one.</p>\n      <div class=\"card\" style=\"padding:16px 24px 8px\"><div class=\"tl\">\n        <div class=\"tl-row\"><span class=\"dot red\"></span><div><div class=\"yr\">2021</div><div class=\"t\">Platinum Equity acquired Solenis and merged in Sigura</div><div class=\"d\">Set today's private owner, and added pool and spa water treatment.</div><span class=\"chip\">Adds pool and spa</span></div></div>\n        <div class=\"tl-row\"><span class=\"dot\"></span><div><div class=\"yr\">July 2023</div><div class=\"t\">Acquired Diversey</div><div class=\"d\">Agreed in March, completed in July. Added cleaning, hygiene and food-safety depth, and turned Solenis into a much broader water and hygiene group.</div><span class=\"chip\">About $4.6 billion</span></div></div>\n        <div class=\"tl-row\"><span class=\"dot\"></span><div><div class=\"yr\">June 2024</div><div class=\"t\">Acquired Lilleborg from Orkla</div><div class=\"d\">Added food and beverage and institutional hygiene presence in Norway.</div><span class=\"chip\">Adds Nordic hygiene</span></div></div>\n        <div class=\"tl-row\"><span class=\"dot\"></span><div><div class=\"yr\">July 2024</div><div class=\"t\">Acquired Predictive Food Safety Solutions</div><div class=\"d\">Added poultry-focused antimicrobial, sanitation and food-safety capability.</div><span class=\"chip\">Adds poultry food safety</span></div></div>\n        <div class=\"tl-row\"><span class=\"dot\"></span><div><div class=\"yr\">November 2024</div><div class=\"t\">Acquired BASF's mining flocculants business</div><div class=\"d\">Expanded the minerals-processing offer.</div><span class=\"chip\">Adds mining flocculants</span></div></div>\n        <div class=\"tl-row\"><span class=\"dot\"></span><div><div class=\"yr\">November 2025</div><div class=\"t\">Acquired NCH Corporation, closed 3 November</div><div class=\"d\">Widened middle-market and light water treatment. Bain Capital and the Levy family took minority stakes at close.</div><span class=\"chip\">Adds middle-market water</span></div></div>\n        <div class=\"tl-row\"><span class=\"dot\"></span><div><div class=\"yr\">April 2026</div><div class=\"t\">Broke ground on a new plant in Beihai, China</div><div class=\"d\">Not a purchase. A 60,000 tonne paper-chemicals plant for western China and Southeast Asia, opening in the second quarter of 2027.</div><span class=\"chip\">RMB 250 million, organic</span></div></div>\n      </div></div>\n      <details class=\"segc\" style=\"margin-top:12px\"><summary><div class=\"top\"><div><div class=\"nm\">Why this run of deals matters</div><div class=\"src\">Click to read</div></div></div><span class=\"more\"><span class=\"lbl\">Why this matters to us</span><span class=\"cv\"></span></span></summary><div class=\"why\">This is not random portfolio drift. It is a company assembling a platform that can sell several related things into the same customer, chemistry, service, monitoring and compliance. For us it means two things. Solenis is no longer a single-industry paper story, so we must speak to the specific route. And a platform built this fast usually has parts that look strong in theory but are still unproven in the field, which is exactly where an outside read of real demand is useful.</div></details>\n\n      <h2>Where Solenis is investing and innovating</h2>\n      <p class=\"muted\">Solenis does not publish a research budget, but its recent moves show real investment in capability, especially around paper packaging and digital monitoring. The chemistry is not the question. Whether customers adopt it, and how fast, is.</p>\n      <div class=\"grid3\">\n        <div class=\"card kv\"><p class=\"label\">Research</p><p class=\"big\">New Delaware centre</p><p>A new global research centre opened in October 2025, alongside a Packaging Center of Excellence that runs recognised packaging tests.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"card kv\"><p class=\"label\">The packaging bet</p><p class=\"big\">Barrier coatings</p><p>A dedicated portfolio, the TopScreen and Contour ranges, that replaces plastic, paraffin wax, silicones and added PFAS, with a public Heidelberg collaboration to apply coatings on the printing press.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"card kv\"><p class=\"label\">How it frames value</p><p class=\"big\">Sustainability</p><p>Solenis reports that 74% of revenue supports customer sustainability goals, claims $349 million of documented customer value, and says 91% of its innovation work targets sustainability or circularity.</p></div>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"implication\"><b>What this means for us.</b> The sustainability figures are useful as a guide to how Solenis wants to talk about value with customers, and where it will justify spending on outside support. They are self-reported, so we treat them as direction, not audited fact. The opening for us is the gap these cannot fill, which is whether real customers are buying the coatings, and which formats move first.</div>\n      <p class=\"srcline\">Sources. Solenis 2025 sustainability report, the Heidelberg barrier-coatings release, the barrier-coatings product pages, and the Packaging Center of Excellence page. The 74%, $349 million and 91% figures are self-reported by Solenis.</p>\n\n      <h2>Where external pressure is visible</h2>\n      <p class=\"muted\">Two public signals show the company is exposed to costs outside its control. Solenis applied a minimum 5% temporary surcharge in the United States from May 2025 because of tariff-driven costs, and a global energy surcharge of 8% to 14% from April 2026 in response to Middle East logistics and energy disruption. At the same time the paper and packaging market in Europe is uneven rather than booming, with weak packaging demand reported at DS Smith in late 2024 and continued pressure in European papermaking through 2025.</p>\n      <div class=\"implication\"><b>What this means for us.</b> We should not pitch a general paper-market recovery story, because the market is not recovering evenly. Any proposal has to be tied to the specific applications where customers are still releasing investment despite the wider strain. That discipline is what makes a proposal credible to someone living the cost pressure.</div>\n      <p class=\"srcline\">Sources. Solenis surcharge announcements, April 2025 and March 2026. Reuters on DS Smith, December 2024. Financial Times on Italian papermaking, March 2025.</p>\n\n      <h2>The rule that sets the timing</h2>\n      <p class=\"muted\">The clearest near-term trigger is the European packaging regulation, the PPWR. It entered into force on 11 February 2025 and generally applies from 12 August 2026, and it restricts PFAS in food-contact packaging once set thresholds are exceeded. This is what pushes brands and converters towards paper and towards PFAS-free coatings, which is the heart of the Solenis packaging offer. Solenis already knows the rule. The useful question is which parts of it actually move customer buying, and for which formats.</p>\n      <p class=\"srcline\">Source. European Commission packaging-waste page. The exact PFAS threshold figures should be legally checked against the regulation text before being quoted numerically in a client document.</p>\n\n      <h2>Where to focus, in order</h2>\n      <p class=\"muted\">Four pieces of work survive a simple test, could Solenis realistically pay for this as a first, bounded project. They are ranked by how close each sits to a real budget today. Open any one to see the decision behind it, the buyer, the signals to listen for, and the smallest version that is still worth buying.</p>\n\n      <details class=\"wedge primary\"><summary><div class=\"wtop\"><span class=\"rank\">1</span><span class=\"wt\">Europe paper-based food-contact packaging, deciding where to focus first</span><span class=\"conf high\">High confidence</span></div><div class=\"wsum\">The strongest opening. A live rule, a visible Solenis product set, an identifiable buyer, and a project small enough to buy.</div><span class=\"wmore\"><span>See the detail</span><span class=\"cv\"></span></span></summary>\n        <div class=\"wbody\">\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">The decision we would support</div><div class=\"v\">Which specific paper-based food-contact applications in Europe Solenis should prioritise over the next 12 to 24 months, and where to lean on converter, printer and brand-owner partnerships.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">The trigger</div><div class=\"v\">The packaging regulation applying from August 2026, and growing customer demand to replace plastic, wax, silicones and added PFAS in food packaging.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">Why getting it wrong is costly</div><div class=\"v\">Spreading effort across too many applications wastes technical and commercial resources. Choosing well pulls through chemistry, testing, converter support and proof of customer value.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">The likely buyer</div><div class=\"v\">The VP or GM for Consumer Solutions across EMEA and Asia, or whoever owns barrier-coatings growth in that route. This role is evidenced in the public record.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">Signals to listen for</div><div class=\"v\">Questions about food-contact compliance, converter qualification bottlenecks, a need to narrow application focus, or uncertainty about where to place commercial-development effort.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">The smallest version worth buying</div><div class=\"v\">A six-week sprint for one buyer, one decision and three applications at most, ending in a clear recommendation on where to win first. It excludes non-European geographies, hygiene, mining, data centres and company-wide strategy.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wask\"><span class=\"k\">Opening question</span>As the August deadline approaches, which food-contact paper formats are hardest to prioritise commercially, because regulation, converter readiness and brand-owner demand are moving at different speeds?</div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n\n      <details class=\"wedge\"><summary><div class=\"wtop\"><span class=\"rank\">2</span><span class=\"wt\">Data-centre liquid-cooling services, deciding how hard to invest</span><span class=\"conf medhigh\">Medium to high</span></div><div class=\"wsum\">A real, current growth area where Solenis is already redeploying its Chem-Aqua capability. Strong, but the buyer is less visible today.</div><span class=\"wmore\"><span>See the detail</span><span class=\"cv\"></span></span></summary>\n        <div class=\"wbody\">\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">The trigger</div><div class=\"v\">Solenis joined Dow's Coolant Care Network for data centres in June 2026 through its Chem-Aqua team, covering fluid management, monitoring, predictive maintenance and direct-to-chip cooling.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">The decision we would support</div><div class=\"v\">How aggressively to invest in target segments, partnerships and service models for data-centre liquid cooling. The problem is ecosystem intelligence and positioning, not core chemistry.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">The likely buyer</div><div class=\"v\">The President of Chem-Aqua and light water treatment, or the industrial-water leader responsible for adjacent growth.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">The smallest version worth buying</div><div class=\"v\">A focused North America and Europe ecosystem scan for one growth decision.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wask\"><span class=\"k\">Opening question</span>Where do you still have the biggest uncertainty in data-centre liquid cooling, the target customer segments, the service packaging, or the ecosystem partners?</div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n\n      <details class=\"wedge\"><summary><div class=\"wtop\"><span class=\"rank\">3</span><span class=\"wt\">Cross-sell after the NCH deal, deciding where it converts</span><span class=\"conf med\">Medium</span></div><div class=\"wsum\">Commercially real, but broader and more integration-heavy than is ideal for a first paid project.</div><span class=\"wmore\"><span>See the detail</span><span class=\"cv\"></span></span></summary>\n        <div class=\"wbody\">\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">The decision we would support</div><div class=\"v\">Where to prioritise cross-sell and bring the portfolios together without overloading the field organisation.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">Why external help may be needed</div><div class=\"v\">Integration teams are often rich in internal data but short on an outside read of where the combined proposition will actually convert.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">The likely buyer</div><div class=\"v\">Integration leadership, selected regional business leaders, or light water treatment commercial leadership.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">The smallest version worth buying</div><div class=\"v\">One region, one route to market, one solution family.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wask\"><span class=\"k\">Opening question</span>Since the NCH deal, where do you believe the combined offer is strongest in theory but still under-proven in the field?</div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n\n      <details class=\"wedge\"><summary><div class=\"wtop\"><span class=\"rank\">4</span><span class=\"wt\">Food safety in protein processing, deciding where to grow</span><span class=\"conf med\">Medium</span></div><div class=\"wsum\">Credible, given the Diversey and Predictive Food Safety capabilities, but the decision space is more varied.</div><span class=\"wmore\"><span>See the detail</span><span class=\"cv\"></span></span></summary>\n        <div class=\"wbody\">\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">The decision we would support</div><div class=\"v\">Which subsegments and use cases to prioritise for differentiated growth in protein processing.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">Why external help may be needed</div><div class=\"v\">The question is not whether Solenis can sell into food processing, but where regulation, customer pain and differentiated value are strongest by subsegment.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wrow\"><div class=\"k\">The likely buyer</div><div class=\"v\">The leader of Food and Beverage solutions in North America, or global food and beverage platform leadership.</div></div>\n          <div class=\"wask\"><span class=\"k\">Opening question</span>Where do you see the biggest gap between your current food-safety capabilities and the customer problems that are now getting budget?</div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n\n      <h2>The first paid project to propose</h2>\n      <div class=\"soft-card\"><p style=\"margin:0\">A six-week study, Europe first, that prioritises paper-based food-contact packaging applications for barrier coatings. One buyer, the VP or GM for Consumer Solutions across EMEA and Asia. Three application clusters at most, for example cupstock, grease-resistant foodservice papers, and one coated paperboard format chosen with the buyer. It would weigh regulatory urgency, converter and printer readiness, customer qualification needs, the competitor and partner picture, switching economics, and the right order to act in. It ends with a clear recommendation on where to win first. It deliberately excludes whole-company strategy, hygiene, mining, data centres, global roll-up, technical reformulation and plant modelling. It is kept to one decision and one buyer, because that is what a company with no prior relationship to us can actually fund.</p></div>\n\n      <h2>What not to say</h2>\n      <p class=\"muted\">Four things would fail the evidence and credibility tests. Do not speak as if Solenis is a publicly reported company with open business-unit financials. Do not claim a formal segment structure that the company does not disclose. Do not imply that the packaging regulation automatically guarantees large near-term revenue. And do not pitch a whole-company strategy study as the first engagement. Each of these would mark us out as not understanding either the company or the rules.</p>\n\n      <h2>What still needs checking before use</h2>\n      <p class=\"muted\">Being straight about what we do not yet know is part of the credibility. The brief flags the following as not reliably public, to be confirmed before any of it is used in a client document.</p>\n      <div class=\"card\" style=\"padding:8px 24px\"><div class=\"vlist\">\n        <div class=\"vitem\"><span class=\"d2\"></span><div><b>Money and structure.</b> Current debt, leverage and any credit rating were not reliably available from high-confidence public sources, and must not be inferred from older drafts or web summaries. Segment profitability and regional revenue mix are not disclosed.</div></div>\n        <div class=\"vitem\"><span class=\"d2\"></span><div><b>The exact rule detail.</b> The exact PFAS threshold figures in the packaging regulation should be legally checked against the regulation text before being quoted numerically.</div></div>\n        <div class=\"vitem\"><span class=\"d2\"></span><div><b>The named buyer.</b> The public record shows a Consumer Solutions regional leadership role, but not a full org chart for who owns barrier-coatings decisions. The specific person and title should be confirmed.</div></div>\n        <div class=\"vitem\"><span class=\"d2\"></span><div><b>How the buyer frames the issue.</b> Whether they see it as regulatory readiness, commercial prioritisation, partnership choice, or innovation sequencing. The project holds across all four, but the opening language should be tuned once this is known.</div></div>\n      </div></div>\n\n      <h2>The signals behind the outreach</h2>\n      <p class=\"muted\">Every email is built on a signal from the brief. Each one below is drawn from a company source, a regulator, or reputable media, so all are publicly confirmed. The relevance bar shows how central each is to the message, and the last column shows which conversation uses it.</p>\n      <div class=\"card\" style=\"padding:8px 24px\">\n        <div class=\"sig\"><div class=\"s\">The packaging rule applies from 12 August 2026<small>Restricts PFAS in food-contact packaging, pushing the switch to paper and PFAS-free coatings</small></div><div class=\"m\"><span class=\"status\"><span class=\"sq\"></span>Confirmed</span></div><div class=\"m\">Packaging, primary<div class=\"bar2\"><i style=\"width:100%\"></i></div></div></div>\n        <div class=\"sig\"><div class=\"s\">A visible barrier-coatings offer, with Heidelberg<small>TopScreen and Contour ranges, plus a public collaboration to coat on the press</small></div><div class=\"m\"><span class=\"status\"><span class=\"sq\"></span>Confirmed</span></div><div class=\"m\">Packaging, primary<div class=\"bar2\"><i style=\"width:95%\"></i></div></div></div>\n        <div class=\"sig\"><div class=\"s\">Joined Dow's data-centre cooling network<small>Chem-Aqua capability redeployed into a growth market in June 2026</small></div><div class=\"m\"><span class=\"status\"><span class=\"sq\"></span>Confirmed</span></div><div class=\"m\">Data centre<div class=\"bar2\"><i style=\"width:88%\"></i></div></div></div>\n        <div class=\"sig\"><div class=\"s\">Built by acquisition, so cross-sell is unproven in the field<small>Diversey, Lilleborg, Predictive Food Safety, BASF flocculants and NCH in three years</small></div><div class=\"m\"><span class=\"status\"><span class=\"sq\"></span>Confirmed</span></div><div class=\"m\">NCH cross-sell<div class=\"bar2\"><i style=\"width:82%\"></i></div></div></div>\n        <div class=\"sig\"><div class=\"s\">Deeper food-safety capability in protein<small>The Predictive Food Safety acquisition added poultry antimicrobial and sanitation</small></div><div class=\"m\"><span class=\"status\"><span class=\"sq\"></span>Confirmed</span></div><div class=\"m\">Food safety<div class=\"bar2\"><i style=\"width:78%\"></i></div></div></div>\n        <div class=\"sig\"><div class=\"s\">Visible cost pressure, and an uneven paper market<small>Tariff and energy surcharges, with weak European packaging demand</small></div><div class=\"m\"><span class=\"status\"><span class=\"sq\"></span>Confirmed</span></div><div class=\"m\">Framing, all<div class=\"bar2\"><i style=\"width:70%\"></i></div></div></div>\n      </div>\n\n      <h2>Recent things worth knowing</h2>\n      <div class=\"card\" style=\"padding:6px 24px\">\n        <div class=\"signal\"><div class=\"when\">10 June 2026</div><p class=\"what\">Joined Dow's Coolant Care Network for data centres through Chem-Aqua, covering fluid management, monitoring and direct-to-chip cooling. A real, current growth move, and the basis of the second wedge.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"signal\"><div class=\"when\">April 2026</div><p class=\"what\">Broke ground on a 60,000 tonne paper-chemicals plant in Beihai, China, with RMB 250 million of investment and start-up in 2027. Firm evidence that growth in Asia is a funded priority.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"signal\"><div class=\"when\">March 2026</div><p class=\"what\">Announced a global energy surcharge of 8% to 14% from April, after a tariff surcharge in the United States the year before. Both confirm exposure to freight, feedstock and energy costs.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"signal\"><div class=\"when\">October 2025</div><p class=\"what\">Opened a new global research centre in Delaware, alongside the Packaging Center of Excellence. Part of presenting Solenis as an integrated solutions partner, not only a supplier of chemicals.</p></div>\n      </div></div>";
ACCOUNT_SOLENIS.wedges = [{"rank":1,"title":"Europe paper-based food-contact packaging, deciding where to focus first","decision":"Which paper-based food-contact applications in Europe to prioritise over the next 12 to 24 months, and where to lean on converter, printer and brand-owner partnerships.","actors":"","timing":"The strongest opening. A live rule, a visible Solenis product set, an identifiable buyer, and a project small enough to buy.","findOut":"A six-week sprint for one buyer, one decision and three applications at most, ending in a clear recommendation on where to win first.","boundary":"","question":"As the August deadline approaches, which food-contact paper formats are hardest to prioritise commercially, because regulation, converter readiness and brand-owner demand are moving at different speeds?"},{"rank":2,"title":"Data-centre liquid-cooling services, deciding how hard to invest","decision":"How aggressively to invest in target segments, partnerships and service models for data-centre liquid cooling.","actors":"","timing":"A real, current growth area where Solenis is already redeploying Chem-Aqua. Strong, but the buyer is less visible today.","findOut":"A focused North America and Europe ecosystem scan for one growth decision.","boundary":"","question":"Where do you still have the biggest uncertainty in data-centre liquid cooling, the target customer segments, the service packaging, or the ecosystem partners?"},{"rank":3,"title":"Cross-sell after the NCH deal, deciding where it converts","decision":"Where to prioritise cross-sell and bring the portfolios together without overloading the field organisation.","actors":"","timing":"Commercially real, but broader and more integration-heavy than is ideal for a first paid project.","findOut":"One region, one route to market, one solution family.","boundary":"","question":"Since the NCH deal, where do you believe the combined offer is strongest in theory but still under-proven in the field?"},{"rank":4,"title":"Food safety in protein processing, deciding where to grow","decision":"Which subsegments and use cases to prioritise for differentiated growth in protein processing.","actors":"","timing":"Credible, given the Diversey and Predictive Food Safety capabilities, but the decision space is more varied.","findOut":"A focused subsegment prioritisation for one route to market.","boundary":"","question":"Where do you see the biggest gap between your current food-safety capabilities and the customer problems that are now getting budget?"}];
ACCOUNT_SOLENIS.profiles = [{"id":"sp_consumer_emea","wedge":1,"role":"VP or GM, Consumer Solutions, EMEA and Asia","owns":"Owns paper and packaging growth, including barrier coatings, across EMEA and Asia. Owns where Solenis places its commercial and technical effort in paper-based packaging across Europe and Asia, including the barrier-coatings growth that replaces plastic and PFAS.","pressures":"Deciding which paper-based food-contact applications to prioritise over the next 12 to 24 months. Regulation, converter readiness and brand-owner demand are moving at different speeds. Knowing where converter and printer partnerships, not chemistry, are the binding constraint.","pains":[{"p":"Deciding which paper-based food-contact applications to prioritise over the next 12 to 24 months.","why":"The brief names this as the core decision for this role. With limited technical and commercial resource, backing too many applications spreads effort thin, while the right few pull through chemistry, testing and converter support."},{"p":"Regulation, converter readiness and brand-owner demand are moving at different speeds.","why":"The packaging rule applies from August 2026, but converters qualify on their own timelines and brand owners place orders on theirs. Knowing which formats are converging fastest is an outside sensing problem, not a lab one."},{"p":"Knowing where converter and printer partnerships, not chemistry, are the binding constraint.","why":"Solenis already has a visible coatings portfolio and a Heidelberg press-applied collaboration. The harder question is which converters can actually run the coating at sensible cost, which the brief flags as the downstream gap."}],"angle":"The packaging rule from August is not one switch but several formats moving at different speeds. The decision is which to back first, and where converter readiness, rather than chemistry, decides the pace.","help":"We map real adoption across the packaging value chain, by format and by converter, so commercial effort backs where demand is actually forming.","link":"Connects to wedge 1, the primary opening.","subject":"Which formats convert first in food-contact packaging","email":"Hi [first name],\n\nIn food-contact packaging, the hard part of the barrier-coatings shift is not the chemistry. It is which formats convert first, and ahead of the August 2026 deadline that answer is forming converter by converter.\n\nThe signal rarely sits in published data. It sits with the converters committing qualification slots, the brand owners setting specifications, and the press manufacturers deciding what to support.\n\nReaching them is what we do at FutureBridge: primary, bottom-up intelligence on the technical and commercial reality before qualification capital is committed.\n\nFor a coatings portfolio, that could mean:\n- Which format and converter combinations become reference positions first?\n- What performance trade-offs will brand owners accept against plastic?\n\nIf this is useful to discuss near-term, we'd welcome a 30-minute meeting at a date and time of your choosing.","inmail":"Hi [first name], in food-contact packaging the hard part of the barrier-coatings shift is which formats convert first, and ahead of the August 2026 deadline that answer is forming converter by converter. It sits with the converters committing qualification slots and the brand owners setting specifications, not in published data. Reaching them is what we do at FutureBridge: primary, bottom-up intelligence before qualification capital is committed. If this is useful to discuss near-term, we'd welcome a 30-minute meeting at a date and time of your choosing.","profSources":[{"txt":"The European packaging rule applies from 12 August 2026 and restricts PFAS in food-contact packaging above set thresholds. The exact thresholds need legal checking before being quoted.","src":"European Commission packaging-waste page","st":"ok"},{"txt":"Solenis has a visible barrier-coatings portfolio, TopScreen and Contour, and a public Heidelberg collaboration to apply coatings on the press.","src":"Solenis barrier-coatings pages; Heidelberg release, 2024","st":"ok"},{"txt":"A VP or GM Consumer Solutions EMEA and Asia role is evidenced in the public record. The specific person and title should be confirmed via People Data Labs.","src":"Solenis Beihai release, April 2026","st":"check"},{"txt":"Which formats are pulling real qualification now, versus stated intent, is the external read we would bring. It is not a stated Solenis fact.","src":"FutureBridge interpretation","st":"interp"}]},{"id":"sp_chemaqua","wedge":2,"role":"President, Chem-Aqua and light water treatment","owns":"Leads light water treatment and the move into data-centre liquid cooling. Leads the light water treatment business that came with NCH, and the move into data-centre liquid cooling through the Chem-Aqua team.","pressures":"Deciding how hard to invest in data-centre liquid cooling, and in which segments. Knowing which customer types, integrators and coolant relationships scale soonest. Positioning a service and monitoring offer while the buying centre is still forming.","pains":[{"p":"Deciding how hard to invest in data-centre liquid cooling, and in which segments.","why":"The brief frames this as the open decision after Solenis joined Dow's Coolant Care Network. The problem is ecosystem intelligence and positioning, not core chemistry."},{"p":"Knowing which customer types, integrators and coolant relationships scale soonest.","why":"The fastest-scaling parts of the market are not always the most visible, and direct-to-chip cooling creates service needs that differ from traditional water treatment."},{"p":"Positioning a service and monitoring offer while the buying centre is still forming.","why":"The brief notes the data-centre buyer is less publicly visible today, so where to aim the offer first is genuinely uncertain."}],"angle":"The useful question in data-centre cooling is not how fast the market grows, but which customers, integrators and coolant relationships convert into steady service work first, which direct-to-chip cooling is reshaping quickly.","help":"We map how new industrial-service ecosystems form, so an offer is aimed where the early, repeatable demand is concentrating.","link":"Connects to wedge 2, data-centre cooling.","subject":"Service models in direct-to-chip cooling","email":"Hi [first name],\n\nIn direct-to-chip cooling, the service model is being shaped early, before the market has settled.\n\nHyperscale operators, colocation providers, OEMs and integrators are each forming views on who should own fluid monitoring, lifecycle assurance and long-term performance risk. Those relationships are being set now.\n\nThe answers rarely sit in published data. They sit with the operators running early deployments and the integrators writing the specs. Reaching them is what we do at FutureBridge: primary, bottom-up intelligence on the technical and commercial reality before service commitments are made.\n\nThe Dow Coolant Care Network positions Chem-Aqua well in that conversation.\n\nIf this is useful to discuss near-term, we'd welcome a 30-minute meeting at a date and time of your choosing.","inmail":"Hi [first name], in direct-to-chip cooling the service model is being shaped early, before the market has settled. Operators, OEMs and integrators are deciding now who owns fluid monitoring and lifecycle assurance, and the answers sit with the people running early deployments, not in published data. Reaching them is what we do at FutureBridge. The Dow Coolant Care Network positions Chem-Aqua well in that conversation. If this is useful to discuss near-term, we'd welcome a 30-minute meeting at a date and time of your choosing.","profSources":[{"txt":"Solenis joined Dow's Coolant Care Network for data centres in June 2026 through Chem-Aqua, covering fluid management, monitoring and direct-to-chip cooling.","src":"Solenis news release, 10 June 2026","st":"ok"},{"txt":"A President of Chem-Aqua and light water treatment role exists following the NCH acquisition. The specific person and title should be confirmed via People Data Labs.","src":"Solenis NCH release, November 2025","st":"check"},{"txt":"Where early service demand concentrates by customer type and partner is the external read we would bring, not a stated Solenis fact.","src":"FutureBridge interpretation","st":"interp"}]},{"id":"sp_fnb","wedge":4,"role":"Leader, Food and Beverage solutions","owns":"Leads food and beverage solutions, including protein-processing food safety. Leads the food and beverage solutions built from Diversey and the Predictive Food Safety acquisition, including antimicrobial and sanitation work in protein processing.","pressures":"Choosing which protein subsegments and use cases to prioritise for differentiated growth. Finding the widest gap between current food-safety capability and problems now getting budget. Avoiding a generic hygiene pitch in a varied decision space.","pains":[{"p":"Choosing which protein subsegments and use cases to prioritise for differentiated growth.","why":"The brief notes the question is not whether Solenis can sell into food processing, but where regulation, customer pain and differentiated value are strongest by subsegment."},{"p":"Finding the widest gap between current food-safety capability and problems now getting budget.","why":"Customer spend in protein is uneven across subsegments, and the Predictive Food Safety acquisition added poultry-specific capability that fits some areas better than others."},{"p":"Avoiding a generic hygiene pitch in a varied decision space.","why":"The brief rates this credible but heterogeneous, so the value is in precise subsegment targeting rather than a broad food-safety message."}],"angle":"In protein processing the movement is in where customer budgets are now being released, which is uneven by subsegment. Poultry looks different from red meat in where a differentiated approach earns a premium.","help":"We map where customer budgets are concentrating by subsegment, so growth effort lands where differentiated value is strongest.","link":"Connects to wedge 4, food safety in protein.","subject":"From testing to assurance in protein processing","email":"Hi [first name],\n\nIn protein processing, the hard part of the shift from periodic testing to continuous assurance is not the technology. It is who specifies the standard, and who holds the data.\n\nRetailers, QSR chains and processors are answering that differently, category by category, and the expectations they set now will decide which suppliers sit inside the assurance conversation.\n\nThe picture rarely sits in published data. It sits with the QA leads, plant operators and procurement teams setting those specifications. Reaching them is what we do at FutureBridge: primary, bottom-up intelligence on the technical and commercial reality before positions harden.\n\nIf this is useful to discuss near-term, we'd welcome a 30-minute meeting at a date and time of your choosing.","inmail":"Hi [first name], in protein processing the shift from periodic testing to continuous assurance turns on who specifies the standard and who holds the data. Retailers and processors are answering that differently, and the expectations set now will decide which suppliers sit inside the assurance conversation. The picture sits with QA leads and procurement teams, not in published data. Reaching them is what we do at FutureBridge. If this is useful to discuss near-term, we'd welcome a 30-minute meeting at a date and time of your choosing.","profSources":[{"txt":"Solenis deepened food and beverage capability through Diversey and the acquisition of Predictive Food Safety Solutions, which added poultry antimicrobial and sanitation capability.","src":"Solenis releases, 2023 and July 2024","st":"ok"},{"txt":"A Food and Beverage solutions leadership role exists across the combined group. The specific person and title should be confirmed via People Data Labs.","src":"Solenis market pages","st":"check"},{"txt":"Where the gap is widest between capability and budgeted customer problems, by subsegment, is the external read we would bring.","src":"FutureBridge interpretation","st":"interp"}]},{"id":"sp_nch_integration","wedge":3,"role":"Commercial leader, post-NCH integration","owns":"Leads cross-sell and portfolio harmonisation after the NCH acquisition. Leads bringing the combined portfolios together after the NCH acquisition, and deciding where cross-sell is worth pushing without overloading the field.","pressures":"Deciding where to prioritise cross-sell without overloading the field organisation. Telling apart propositions that convert quickly from those that only look strong on paper. Sequencing cross-sell by region and route to market.","pains":[{"p":"Deciding where to prioritise cross-sell without overloading the field organisation.","why":"The brief names this as the core post-NCH decision. Pushing every combined proposition at once stretches the field and slows everything down."},{"p":"Telling apart propositions that convert quickly from those that only look strong on paper.","why":"Integration teams are rich in internal data but short on an external read of where the combined offer actually converts, which the brief flags directly."},{"p":"Sequencing cross-sell by region and route to market.","why":"The brief's smallest version of this work is one region, one route, one solution family, which implies the real difficulty is choosing where to start."}],"angle":"After several acquisitions, the pattern is strong internal data on what the combined business can sell, but a thinner read on where it is actually converting. Some propositions pull quickly, others look good on paper but stay slow.","help":"We bring an outside read on where a combined offer is meeting real demand, by region and route, so cross-sell starts where the pull is real.","link":"Connects to wedge 3, cross-sell after NCH.","subject":"The cross-sell window after NCH","email":"Hi [first name],\n\nAfter most industrial-services combinations, the cross-sell map is set in the first wave of accounts, long before integration is complete. Customers re-tender, competitors target the overlap, and the early accounts served well become the internal proof.\n\nWhich segments and site types to prove the combined offer in first is a live choice, and the window is measured in quarters.\n\nThe answer rarely sits in published data. It sits with the site engineers, distributors and procurement teams deciding whether one combined supplier is simpler or riskier. Reaching them is what we do at FutureBridge: primary, bottom-up intelligence before account priorities are fixed.\n\nIf this is useful to discuss near-term, we'd welcome a 30-minute meeting at a date and time of your choosing.","inmail":"Hi [first name], after most industrial-services combinations the cross-sell map is set in the first wave of accounts, long before integration is complete. Which segments and site types to prove the combined offer in first is a live choice, and the answer sits with site engineers, distributors and procurement teams, not in published data. Reaching them is what we do at FutureBridge. If this is useful to discuss near-term, we'd welcome a 30-minute meeting at a date and time of your choosing.","profSources":[{"txt":"Solenis completed the NCH acquisition in November 2025 and described it as creating a more diversified company with cross-selling opportunities.","src":"Solenis NCH release, November 2025","st":"ok"},{"txt":"An integration or light water treatment commercial leadership role exists post-NCH. The specific person and title should be confirmed via People Data Labs.","src":"Solenis NCH release, November 2025","st":"check"},{"txt":"Where the combined offer is converting in the field, versus in theory, is the external read we would bring.","src":"FutureBridge interpretation","st":"interp"}]}];
ACCOUNT_SOLENIS.sources = [["S1","Solenis is privately held and owned by Platinum Equity since 2021. Bain Capital and the Levy family hold minority stakes since the NCH deal.","Solenis Our History; NCH completion release",""],["S2","Public scale: more than 23,000 employees, 78 manufacturing facilities, and operations in more than 160 countries.","Solenis Quick Facts",""],["S3","Diversey was acquired for about 4.6 billion dollars, agreed March 2023 and completed July 2023.","Solenis Diversey completion release",""],["S4","NCH Corporation was acquired in November 2025. The price was not disclosed.","Solenis NCH completion release",""],["S5","Other acquisitions: Lilleborg (June 2024), Predictive Food Safety Solutions (July 2024), and BASF mining flocculants (November 2024).","Solenis news releases, 2024",""],["S6","Beihai, China plant: 60,000 tonnes a year, RMB 250 million, start-up in the second quarter of 2027.","Solenis Beihai release, April 2026",""],["S7","The European packaging rule entered into force 11 February 2025 and generally applies from 12 August 2026, restricting PFAS in food-contact packaging above set thresholds.","European Commission packaging-waste page",""],["S8","The exact PFAS threshold figures must be legally checked against the regulation text before being quoted numerically.","PPWR manual check | manual check",""],["S9","Solenis has a barrier-coatings portfolio, the TopScreen and Contour ranges, and a public Heidelberg collaboration to apply coatings on the press.","Solenis barrier-coatings pages; Heidelberg release, 2024",""],["S10","Solenis joined Dow's Coolant Care Network for data centres in June 2026 through Chem-Aqua.","Solenis news release, 10 June 2026",""],["S11","Cost pressure: a minimum 5% US surcharge from May 2025, and a global 8% to 14% energy surcharge from April 2026.","Solenis surcharge announcements, 2025 and 2026",""],["S12","The European paper and packaging market is uneven: weak packaging demand at DS Smith in late 2024 and continued pressure in European papermaking in 2025.","Reuters, December 2024; Financial Times, March 2025",""],["S13","Self-reported sustainability figures: 74% of revenue supports customer sustainability goals, 349 million dollars of documented customer value, and 91% of innovation work on sustainability or circularity. Treat as direction, not audited fact.","Solenis 2025 sustainability report | interpretation",""],["S14","Current debt, leverage and any credit rating were not reliably available from high-confidence public sources, and must not be inferred from older drafts or web summaries.","Brief manual-check note | manual check",""],["S15","Segment profitability, regional revenue mix, and named budget owners below the C-suite are not publicly disclosed. Treat as discovery questions.","Brief evidence-discipline note | manual check",""],["S16","The specific person who owns Europe barrier-coatings growth should be confirmed through People Data Labs before any name is used.","Brief manual-check note; People Data Labs | manual check",""]];
ACCOUNT_SOLENIS.evidenceRules = {"loadBearing":["Privately held, owned by Platinum Equity since 2021; Bain Capital and the Levy family hold minority stakes since the NCH deal.","Public scale: more than 23,000 employees, 78 manufacturing facilities, operations in more than 160 countries.","Diversey acquired for about $4.6 billion, completed July 2023; NCH Corporation completed November 2025, price not disclosed.","Lilleborg (June 2024), Predictive Food Safety Solutions (July 2024) and BASF mining flocculants (November 2024) acquisitions.","The European packaging regulation entered into force 11 February 2025 and generally applies from 12 August 2026, restricting PFAS in food-contact packaging above set thresholds.","Barrier-coatings portfolio (TopScreen and Contour) with a public Heidelberg press-applied collaboration.","Joined Dow's Coolant Care Network for data centres in June 2026 through Chem-Aqua.","Cost pressure: minimum 5% US surcharge from May 2025; global 8% to 14% energy surcharge from April 2026."],"neverQuote":["Current debt, leverage or any credit rating. Not reliably public; never inferred from older drafts or web summaries.","Exact PFAS threshold numbers. The figures must be legally checked against the regulation text before numeric use.","Any claim that presents Solenis as a publicly reported company with open business-unit financials, or a formal segment structure it does not disclose.","Any implication that the packaging regulation automatically guarantees large near-term revenue.","Named budget owners below the C-suite. Not publicly disclosed; treat as discovery questions."],"bannedNumbers":["Any PFAS threshold figure (legal check required first)","Any debt, leverage or credit-rating figure (not reliably public)","The 74% / $349 million / 91% sustainability figures stated as audited fact (self-reported direction only)"]};
ACCOUNT_SOLENIS.modelContext = "ACCOUNT IN BRIEF: Solenis is a privately held, Platinum Equity-owned platform that combines water treatment, hygiene, process chemistry, digital monitoring and application-specific solutions, built up through the Diversey and NCH acquisitions. It is not a generic specialty chemicals supplier, and it does not publish business-unit financials. The strongest near-term opening is European paper-based food-contact packaging and barrier coatings, tied to the EU packaging rule that applies from 12 August 2026. Secondary openings are data-centre liquid cooling through Chem-Aqua, food safety in protein processing, and cross-sell after the NCH acquisition. The value we add is an outside read on where real customer demand is forming, by application, segment and region. Do not state current debt, leverage or a credit rating, do not quote exact PFAS threshold numbers, and do not treat Solenis as a publicly reported company.\nWedges, ranked: 1. Europe paper-based food-contact packaging, deciding where to focus first (Which paper-based food-contact applications in Europe to prioritise over the next 12 to 24 months, and where to lean on converter, printer and brand-owner partnerships.) | 2. Data-centre liquid-cooling services, deciding how hard to invest (How aggressively to invest in target segments, partnerships and service models for data-centre liquid cooling.) | 3. Cross-sell after the NCH deal, deciding where it converts (Where to prioritise cross-sell and bring the portfolios together without overloading the field organisation.) | 4. Food safety in protein processing, deciding where to grow (Which subsegments and use cases to prioritise for differentiated growth in protein processing.)\nHARD BOUNDARIES: never state current debt, leverage or a credit rating. Never quote exact PFAS threshold numbers. Never present Solenis as a publicly reported company or claim a formal segment structure it does not disclose. Never imply the packaging regulation guarantees large near-term revenue. Treat the 74%, $349 million and 91% sustainability figures as self-reported direction, not audited fact. Named budget owners below the C-suite are not public; treat them as discovery questions. The four recommended profiles are roles evidenced in the public record, not confirmed individuals: verify the person before sending.";
